[0m[0m
There were [1mtwo bugs[22m in the Rust translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Missing exported symbol ([38;5;10mlib.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The test runner dynamically loads [38;5;10mlibblake.so[0m and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m. The Rust code had [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m as an internal function in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m but never exported it as a C-compatible symbol.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Added a [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_gen_message_random(...)[0m wrapper in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m that converts raw pointers to slices and delegates to [38;5;10mhash::gen_message_random[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: Incorrect bit-length arguments to [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m ([38;5;10mhash.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m takes its [38;5;10mdatalen[0m parameter in [1mbits[22m, not bytes. The C [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m functions call [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m passing [38;5;10mSPX_N[0m (24) directly — meaning 24 bits = 3 bytes of data.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation incorrectly multiplied by 8 ([38;5;10mSPX_N * 8 = 192[0m bits = 24 bytes), which hashed far more data than the C code intended.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is a subtle API mismatch: the [38;5;10mblake512()[0m convenience wrapper takes byte lengths and multiplies by 8 internally, but [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m call [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m directly, passing the raw value that the C code passes — which happens to be the byte count used as a bit count.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Removed the [38;5;10m* 8[0m multiplications in both [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m, passing [38;5;10mSPX_N as u64[0m, [38;5;10mSPX_PK_BYTES as u64[0m, and [38;5;10mmlen[0m directly to [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m, matching the C code exactly.[0m[0m
